<Layout
  title="DeepSeek Flash - A.I. - Rycode"
  description="Current recommended DeepSeek API model. API id deepseek-flash. Model version DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash-1017, released 17 Oct 2026."
  variant="ai"
>
  <article class="ai-model">
    <p class="ai-model-kicker">DeepSeek · current</p>
    <h1 class="ai-model-title">DeepSeek Flash (V4.1-Flash-1017)</h1>
    <p class="ai-model-dek">DeepSeek Flash is DeepSeek's current recommended API model. The API id is <code>deepseek-flash</code>, and since 17 Oct 2026 the pricing page lists the model version as DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash-1017. DeepSeek's Hugging Face card calls it "a minor update" to DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash. DeepSeek says it scores higher on benchmarks, finishes tasks faster and writes shorter outputs than the previous version. It is live on DeepSeek's website, app and API. The legacy names <code>deepseek-v4-flash</code> and <code>deepseek-v4-flash-vision-exp</code> still route here and bill at Flash prices. The 10 Sep version is in the <a href="/ai/models/deepseek/v4-1-flash/">archive</a>.</p>
    <hr class="ai-model-rule" />
    <ModelFacts />
    <h2 class="ai-model-section">Training</h2>
    <p>DeepSeek says the update uses reinforcement learning (training by trial and reward) with self-feedback to get better at building whole projects, from games to 3D models. It does not publish a new parameter count for this version. The 10 Sep base model is described on the <a href="/ai/models/deepseek/v4-1-flash/">archived card</a>.</p>
    <h2 class="ai-model-section">Inference</h2>
    <p>Thinking mode (the model reasons step by step before answering) is on by default, and non-thinking mode is available. Vision (reading images), tool calls, JSON output, the Responses API and an Anthropic-compatible API are supported. FIM completion (fill in the middle of code) is non-thinking only and in beta. DeepSeek says it tuned the model for DeepSeek Harness, Tencent CodeBuddy and WorkBuddy, and recommends DeepSeek Harness 0.2.1-rc.1 or later.</p>
    <h2 class="ai-model-section">Context</h2>
    <p>The context window (how much text the model can read at once) is 1 million tokens. Max output is 384K tokens on the pricing page.</p>
    <h2 class="ai-model-section">Benchmarking</h2>
    <p>The table below holds DeepSeek's own numbers from its 17 Oct 2026 changelog and Hugging Face card. No independent group has re-run those tests.</p>
    <p>Artificial Analysis (AA), an independent group that runs the same set of tests on every model, scores V4.1-Flash-1017 at max effort at <strong>45</strong> on its Intelligence Index v4.3.2 (posted 18 Oct 2026, fetched 19 Oct 2026). The 10 Sep version scored 39. That puts it second among open-weight models on AA's leaderboard, one point behind MiMo-V2.6-Pro at 46. AA's index is a separate composite score, not a re-run of DeepSeek's table.</p>
    <div class="ai-model-table-wrap">
      <table class="ai-model-table ai-model-table--prose">
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Benchmark</th><th scope="col">V4.1-Flash-1017</th><th scope="col">V4.1-Flash (10 Sep)</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>HLE, no tools ¹</td><td>42.6 (44.0 text only) <span class="ai-model-facts-sub">DeepSeek</span></td><td>36.8 (39.1 text only) <span class="ai-model-facts-sub">DeepSeek</span></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Terminal-Bench 4.0 ²</td><td>42.3 <span class="ai-model-facts-sub">DeepSeek</span></td><td>31.2 <span class="ai-model-facts-sub">DeepSeek</span></td></tr>
          <tr><td>DeepSWE v1.1 ²</td><td>77.0 <span class="ai-model-facts-sub">DeepSeek</span></td><td>74.2 <span class="ai-model-facts-sub">DeepSeek</span></td></tr>
          <tr><td>ProgramBench</td><td>29.1 <span class="ai-model-facts-sub">DeepSeek</span></td><td>20.3 <span class="ai-model-facts-sub">DeepSeek</span></td></tr>
          <tr><td>NL2Repo-Bench</td><td>68.2 <span class="ai-model-facts-sub">DeepSeek</span></td><td>65.4 <span class="ai-model-facts-sub">DeepSeek</span></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Automation-Bench v1.0.6</td><td>60.5 <span class="ai-model-facts-sub">DeepSeek</span></td><td>54.8 <span class="ai-model-facts-sub">DeepSeek</span></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Agents' Last Exam</td><td>35.4 <span class="ai-model-facts-sub">DeepSeek</span></td><td>31.8 <span class="ai-model-facts-sub">DeepSeek</span></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Apex Bench v1.1 ⁴</td><td>72.1 <span class="ai-model-facts-sub">DeepSeek</span></td><td>64.6 <span class="ai-model-facts-sub">DeepSeek</span></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Chartography, with tools</td><td>83.0 <span class="ai-model-facts-sub">DeepSeek</span></td><td>78.9 <span class="ai-model-facts-sub">DeepSeek</span></td></tr>
          <tr><td>BabyVision, with tools</td><td>92.5 <span class="ai-model-facts-sub">DeepSeek</span></td><td>89.6 <span class="ai-model-facts-sub">DeepSeek</span></td></tr>
          <tr><td>ZeroBench-main, with tools ³</td><td>51.0 <span class="ai-model-facts-sub">DeepSeek</span></td><td>49.0 <span class="ai-model-facts-sub">DeepSeek</span></td></tr>
        </tbody></table></div>
    <p class="ai-lab-table-note">¹ "Text only" is the text subset of HLE. ² DeepSeek ran Terminal-Bench 4.0 in the Claude Code harness and DeepSWE v1.1 in the Mini-SWE-V2 harness. ³ ZeroBench-main is scored Pass@5 (best of five tries). ⁴ The whole 10 Sep column comes from DeepSeek's 17 Oct comparison table. The Apex Bench figure does not appear in DeepSeek's 10 Sep changelog.</p>
    <h2 class="ai-model-section">Weights and license</h2>
    <p>Open weights (files you can download and run yourself) are on <a href="https://huggingface.co/deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash-1017">Hugging Face</a> and ModelScope under the MIT license. Hugging Face lists the weights in mixed precision, including FP8 (8-bit numbers). Hugging Face counts about 763B stored parameters, while DeepSeek's 10 Sep announce gave 552B. No DeepSeek source explains the difference.</p>
    <h2 class="ai-model-section">Access and pricing</h2>
    <p>Base URL is <code>https://api.deepseek.com</code> (OpenAI format) or <code>https://api.deepseek.com/anthropic</code>. DeepSeek says API calls and pricing are unchanged by the upgrade. Off-peak prices are half of peak. Per 1M tokens on the pricing page:</p>
    <div class="ai-model-table-wrap">
      <table class="ai-model-table ai-model-table--prose">
        <thead><tr><th scope="col"></th><th scope="col">Off-peak</th><th scope="col">Peak</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Input cache hit</td><td>$0.003</td><td>$0.006</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Input cache miss</td><td>$0.15</td><td>$0.30</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Output</td><td>$0.60</td><td>$1.20</td></tr>
        </tbody></table></div>
    <p>Peak hours (docs): 01:00 to 04:00 and 06:00 to 10:00 UTC, Monday through Friday, excluding Chinese public holidays. Concurrency limit listed: 2500.</p>
